@scene(100)

// A BOLTED JOINT, one call each: an ISO 4762 M6 cap screw clamping a lid to a
// base, its head down in a counterbore and its thread in a tapped hole.
//
// joint.bolted TAKES THE BODIES AND HANDS THEM BACK FINISHED. Given the part
// it clamps, the part it threads into, the seat, the direction the shank runs,
// the size, the grip and the fit, it returns the screw, the clamped part with
// its counterbore and ISO 273 clearance cut, and the threaded part with its
// tapped hole cut — every feature from one frame and one size, so nothing has
// to be kept in step by hand. It picks the screw's length too: the shortest
// ISO 4762 length that threads 1.5 diameters past the grip, an M6 x 20 here.
//
// The right-hand joint sits square to a 30-degree face; the only difference
// between it and the upright one is the seat and direction it is handed.
//
// THE THREADS ARE RELIEF. The screw is the smooth shank at the major diameter
// and the base is bored at the basic minor, their maximum-material forms, and
// the joint puts each thread on its part's material. They interfere by the
// annulus between those diameters where they share length — the engagement —
// which is designed, and which the joint's claims allow for by name.
//
// PROVE IT WITH verifyJoints, which finds both joints and proves, for each over
// its own region, that the screw clears the lid, overlaps the base only in its
// thread, that the lid fills the grip under the head, that the base holds 1.5 d
// of thread with a wall round it, and that the screw clears everything else
// returned. A grip that is not the parts' grip, or a base too thin round the
// thread, comes back disproven.
//
// The lids and bases are CUT AWAY in front of y = 0 to show the joint; the
// screws are whole.

aluminumMat, aluminumColor = metal.aluminum();
steelMat, steelColor = metal.steel();

d = 6.0;
fit = fastener.fitMedium;

// The lid under the head is `grip` thick, and the counterbore takes the 6 tall
// head half a millimetre below the lid's face.
grip = 7.5;
cbDepth = 6.5;

// The bodies before the joint, in its frame: seating plane z = 0, shank down.
lidLocal = box([-15.0, -15.0, 0.0 - grip], [15.0, 15.0, cbDepth]);
baseLocal = box([-15.0, -15.0, -30.0], [15.0, 15.0, 0.0 - grip]);

// The upright joint: both bodies placed by the seat's own frame.
seat1 = [-22.0, 0.0, 0.0];
dir1 = [0.0, 0.0, -1.0];
at1 = fastener.seatedAt(seat1, dir1);
screw1, lidWhole1, baseWhole1 = joint.bolted(remap(lidLocal, at1), remap(baseLocal, at1),
                                             seat1, dir1, d, grip, fit);

// The tilted joint: a block whose top is a 30-degree face, built in the WORLD,
// with the screw driven square into that face. The seat is the lid's thickness
// out along the face's normal; the shank runs back along the normal's reverse.
faceN = [0.5, 0.0, 0.8660254];
facePt = [24.0, 0.0, 0.0];
seat2 = facePt + faceN * grip;
dir2 = neg(faceN);
block = max(box([6.0, -15.0, -30.0], [42.0, 15.0, 20.0]), dot(p - facePt, faceN));
screw2, lidWhole2, baseWhole2 = joint.bolted(remap(lidLocal, fastener.seatedAt(seat2, dir2)), block,
                                             seat2, dir2, d, grip, fit);

// The cutaway: everything in front of y = 0 is taken off the lids and bases.
cutaway = neg(p.y);
lid1 = max(lidWhole1, cutaway);
lid2 = max(lidWhole2, cutaway);
base1 = max(baseWhole1, cutaway);
base2 = max(baseWhole2, cutaway);

// The lids are aluminium. The bases keep the joint's thread, but a base is
// tapped first and cut away after, the way it is made: its height is the cut
// base less the cut THREADED base, so the renderer marches exactly the tapped
// base cut at y = 0. The joint's relief laid straight on the cut base would
// push the cut face back by its helix.
pitchVal = thread.metricPitch(d);
female = thread.femaleRelief(d, pitchVal, 0.0 - grip,
                             0.0 - (joint.boltedLength(d, grip) + 3.0 * pitchVal));
lid1.material = aluminumMat;
lid1.color = aluminumColor;
lid2.material = aluminumMat;
lid2.color = aluminumColor;
tapped1 = base1 - max(baseWhole1 - remap(female, at1), cutaway);
tapped2 = base2 - max(baseWhole2 - remap(female, fastener.seatedAt(seat2, dir2)), cutaway);
base1.material = material.withDisplacement(steelMat, tapped1);
base1.color = thread.reliefColor([0.36, 0.38, 0.42], tapped1, pitchVal);
base2.material = material.withDisplacement(steelMat, tapped2);
base2.color = thread.reliefColor([0.36, 0.38, 0.42], tapped2, pitchVal);

return screw1, lid1, base1, screw2, lid2, base2;
